   "text": "I read the structure in three parts and joined them into one SMILES.\n\n**Left part.** A 6-(trifluoromethyl)pyridine-2-carboxamide is attached through its amide NH to C5 of a 2H-indazole. The indazole carries a 6-methoxy group and a 3-methyl group, and its N2 is bonded to a cyclohexane ring.\n\n**Linker.**\n- The cyclohexane is 1,4-disubstituted, with the indazole N on one carbon and a CH2 on the other.\n- The CH2 leads to the nitrogen of a 7-azaspiro[3.5]nonane, i.e. a piperidine spiro-fused to a cyclobutane.\n- The cyclobutane carbon opposite the spiro centre carries a CH2CH2NH chain.\n\n**Right part.** The NH is attached to the 4-position of a phthalimide (isoindoline-1,3-dione). The imide nitrogen carries a 2,6-dioxopiperidin-3-yl group (the thalidomide-type unit).\n\n**Stereochemistry.** The cyclohexane has a wedge on one 1,4-carbon and a hash on the other, which makes it trans. Because the two carbons are related by symmetry, only this relative configuration is defined, so I encoded it as `[C@H]...[C@@H]`. The glutarimide CH and the cyclobutane CH are drawn with plain bonds, so they get no stereo marks.\n\nSMILES: COc1cc2nn([C@H]3CC[C@@H](CC3)CN4CCC5(CC4)CC(C5)CCNc6cccc7C(=O)N(C8CCC(=O)NC8=O)C(=O)c67)c(C)c2cc1NC(=O)c1cccc(n1)C(F)(F)F"
  }
